WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.760
Welcome, everyone.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.290
Thanks for being here.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:12.830
I get to talk to you about
a package called censored.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:15.890
It's a new tidymodels package for survival models.

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.620
And when people hear
survival analysis,

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:23.150
they often think of
situations and data like this.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.220
And I mean, it's called
survival analysis for a reason.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:32.689
Most examples are
indeed of the category time

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:34.070
to death.

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.410
That is not the only
context in which

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:38.810
you might encounter
censored data and hence

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:41.090
a need for survival models.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:46.490
I came across my favorite
example for censored data:

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:48.980
Before I joined RStudio, I
was working at a data science

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.130
consultancy and one
of my colleagues

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:54.900
chatted to me about
his latest project.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.300
And that involved the
newspaper company,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.480
which wanted to know how
many copies they should

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.820
deliver to various outlets.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:07.740
And he was sort of musing that
if there are any copies left,

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.390
at the end of the
day, they would

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:12.180
know how many had been taken and
what the demand for the paper

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.110
was.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:17.110
If there were none left,
they would also still know

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:19.300
how many were taken,
but they wouldn't really

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.560
know what the demand was and how
many more could have, would have been

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.130
taken if they hadn't run out.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.620
So in that case, their
observation of the newspaper

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:29.952
demand

00:01:29.952 --> 00:01:30.910
is actually censored.

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:34.990
It's capped at the number
of papers that they provided.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:38.440
So the stats nerd in me
grinned and was like,

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.720
I suggest you look
at survival analysis.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.630
And now I get to tell you
basically the same thing, too.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:48.500
So if you have data of this
format where you're observing

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.430
something until an event,

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:54.460
be that time to
death or newspapers

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.200
taken until demand saturation,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.260
and you may or may not
actually observe that event.

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:04.660
Please use a survival
analysis! That

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:08.330
is designed to take both of
these aspects into account.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:09.880
So if you take that
data and shove it

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.930
into a regular regression
model and model

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:18.250
a time or the demand without
taking that event indicator

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:22.030
into account, you're treating
the observations as complete

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:25.420
when in fact, they may
just be incomplete.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.540
And if you're just dropping all
of your censored observations

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.530
and model the rest, you're
also throwing away information

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.880
and you're treating these
observations as missing

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.690
while they are actually
just incomplete.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.780
So survival analysis is an
important field of modeling

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.440
and we're extending support
for it in tidymodels.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.260
The first milestone in this

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:52.550
are the models themselves:
to be able to specify them,

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:54.800
fit them, and predict with them.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.800
And you may well
be aware that R has

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.370
an abundance of riches in the
different models contributed

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:03.750
by different people.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.210
And it also has nearly
the same abundance

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:11.240
of riches in interfaces
to these models.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.800
And as you just heard, tidymodels

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.520
wants to make it effective,
safe and ergonomic for you.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:17.990
Part of that is to provide you

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:20.590
with a consistent interface.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:22.330
THe aspect of tidymodels that takes

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:25.820
care of a consistent interface
for models themselves

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:27.330
is parsnip.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.670
It's designed to
give you consistency,

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:33.590
both in how you specify
and fit the model.

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:37.460
And how you predict
and what you get back.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:41.480
So you don't have to remember
function names, argument names

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:44.450
across different
packages, whether or not

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:47.640
you get a vector back or
a matrix or a data frame.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.290
And what that type
of prediction was

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:53.230
called in this particular
package you're using.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:57.160
And the thing I'm really
here to talk about

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:01.310
is censored, which is a
parsnip extension package

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:02.900
for survival models.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:06.490
So I'll be talking about how
these design ideas play out

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:09.440
and marry with survival analysis.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:15.450
Let's get to that first part
to specify and fit models.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:19.660
In parsnip, you need three
elements to specify your model.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:22.360
So for one, the model
type. In the example here,

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:24.280
it's a random forest.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:26.360
And you need a
computational engine.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.450
That's what you use
to fit the model.

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:31.610
In my example here that's 
the ranger package.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.870
Often that's one of various R packages.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.450
It can also be a tool
outside of R, like stan

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:39.320
if you have a Bayesian
model you want to fit.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:42.950
And the third element to a
parsnip model specification

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:44.910
is the mode.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:48.180
A random forest you can use for
regression or classification.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:51.990
So you need to
specify that here.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:54.840
And these three elements.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.290
They are very
consistent in parsnip.

00:04:57.290 --> 00:05:01.360
So they will also
show up in censored.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:04.000
So what is actually
new in censored?

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.530
Well, we had to
make a few additions.

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:09.050
For one, we have
a new model type

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:11.550
called proportional hazards

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:15.640
to let you fit everyone's
favorite Cox model.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.770
Second of all, we
have a new mode

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:22.440
to distinguish censored
regression from regression.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.540
And thirdly, you
probably guessed

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.110
it, several new engines
to existing models.

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:31.980
So with that censored
covers parametric models,

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.260
semi-parametric models
and tree-based models

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:35.880
for survival analysis.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.190
And the last point that
I want to mention here

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:40.530
is that we have a
formula interface

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.390
for all of these models.

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:45.240
Most of the engines
do that already.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.780
The glmnet package sort
of famously does not.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:51.300
But we let you interact
with that through a formula

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.220
interface, and
that includes a way

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.850
to specify stratification.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:06:00.270
And that's all sort of abstract.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:02.290
And I wanted to
have a data example.

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:05.580
Sadly, I do not have access
to that newspaper data.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.760
But I bring you
dolphins and whales.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:13.080
So these are dolphins and whales
living in captivity in the US,

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.380
whether they've been
captured or rescued

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.240
or were born in captivity.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:23.160
It's originally a Tidy
Tuesday data set all the way

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:24.940
from The Pudding.

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:28.990
And we have the response
age in the first column.

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:35.000
How old that dolphin is
or was. If event is zero

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.210
the dolphin or
whale is still alive.

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:39.470
We know the species, the sex

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:41.650
and how many times
this animal was

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:44.580
transferred between different
facilities in the US.

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:49.420
So if you want to
go fit that brand

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:53.890
new model, the proportional
hazards model in tidymodels,

00:06:53.890 --> 00:06:57.970
we need censored and we need the
proportional hazards function.

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:00.730
And then we can add an engine.

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:03.790
I'm going to go with the
very classic survival package

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:06.460
here as the way
to fit this model.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:07.810
We're going to add the mode.

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:10.030
That's the new censored
regression mode.

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:12.110
And with that, I have
the specification.

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:13.400
This is not fit yet.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:17.610
If I want to apply it to
data, I have a fit function.

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:20.290
And that's where the formula
interface comes into play.

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:24.120
Your response is typically a
Surv object on the left hand

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:26.990
side of the formula.

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:30.860
And you want to
add stratification.

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:34.700
You can do that like you would
do in the survival package

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:39.243
and add a strata term
into the formula.

00:07:39.243 --> 00:07:40.910
You're looking at
this and saying, well,

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:44.560
that's a bunch of lines
of code for a model.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:45.680
That is indeed true.

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:50.470
So if you work with the
survival package directly,

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:53.370
that's all a little
bit condensed.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:55.890
Just the modeling
function, the formula

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:58.770
and the data that sort of
does specification and fit all

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:00.780
rolled into one.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:04.810
And that's great.

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:07.630
Where censored comes more

00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:11.590
into play is if you want to
use something else in addition

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:16.180
to try it out and move
between different models.

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:21.160
So if we want to go from the
standard proportional hazards

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:25.390
model to a regularized
version of that model,

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:27.490
I'm going to move all
of the code to the left

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:31.750
and start doing that with the
glmnet package on the right.

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:34.679
That one has no formula
interface, as I mentioned.

00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:38.350
So you need to make
your response matrix, 

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:41.950
a predictor matrix.

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:44.300
I'm doing that with
model.matrix here.

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:47.210
And if you want to
include stratification,

00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:49.430
you need to stratify
your response

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:51.610
and then you're ready to go.

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:55.140
I put it into glmnet. Remember
to set the family to Cox.

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:57.430
Otherwise, you're
fitting a different model.

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:01.530
And give it a penalty
parameter, lambda.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:05.020
So of course, this is doable.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:06.490
But the code looks
quite different

00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:10.230
and it's quite a lot of work
to go from one to another.

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:13.890
And we want to make
that aspect easier.

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:17.430
So obviously the next
code is a little boring.

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:20.730
You can take the bit that
we already had for censored.

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:22.020
And copy it over.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:27.710
And then the main thing you
change is what engine you set.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:29.600
One is the survival package.

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:30.650
One is glmnet.

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:34.010
And the other thing
you want to change

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.890
is the penalty parameter
because it's glmnet,

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:38.930
but the rest is the same.

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:41.600
So we don't have to fiddle
with the data structure here.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:45.520
We can still use a
formula interface.

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:49.200
And if you want to switch
to yet another type

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.840
the tagline for that is kind
of "more models, same syntax".

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:55.830
So you might swap out your
model type for a boosted tree

00:09:55.830 --> 00:10:00.180
and set the engine to mboost
and you're ready to go.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:03.060
And that applies to the rest.

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:07.110
We have in censored, for that
new mode censored regression,

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:10.110
the parametric models
in the survival_reg

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:14.340
function, proportional
hazards for semi-parametric

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:16.740
and then the tree
based models are

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:22.100
decision trees, random forests,
bagged trees and boosted trees.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:26.900
And that's the section on
specifying and fitting.

00:10:26.900 --> 00:10:30.370
And the next one is
obviously predicting.

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:32.860
And in the tidymodels framework,

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:37.060
we give you a little guarantee
that your predictions

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:39.580
will always be inside a tibble.

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:41.410
And the column
names and types are

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:44.770
unsurprising and
predictable, and the number

00:10:44.770 --> 00:10:48.585
of rows in new_data and
the output are the same.

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:54.250
So what's new then?

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:56.140
The answer is new prediction types.

00:10:56.140 --> 00:11:00.590
One of them for survival
analysis is the survival time.

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:02.600
So I'm just taking the
first three rows here

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:06.260
from my data set to predict them
adding an NA in the first row

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:08.720
just to mix things up a little.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:11.630
And you can get a prediction
of the survival time

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:15.590
by using the predict function
and setting type to time

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:17.060
and you get a tibble back.

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:20.420
The column will always be
dot pred underscore time

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:24.200
and it will have three rows
because you put three rows in.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:27.350
So if you have a missing
value, it will have an NA

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:31.580
rather than miraculously
disappear on you.

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:37.460
And the other big new type
for survival predictions

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:42.080
is the survival probability.

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:44.990
That is always calculated
at a point in time.

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:49.460
So here in this example,
I'm setting type survival.

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:51.380
And I want the
survival probability

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:56.330
at age ten, 15, 20 and
40 years for the dolphin or whale

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:58.360
that I'm looking at.

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:00.580
And what do you get back?

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:02.920
It's still a tibble
that has three

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:05.950
rows, for the 3 observations.

00:12:05.950 --> 00:12:07.850
But we've asked for four time points.

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:10.150
So this is actually
a list column

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:13.150
and it has little tibbles
stashed inside of it.

00:12:13.150 --> 00:12:15.310
If we want to look at one of them,

00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:16.340
we can pull it out.

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:18.970
And then we see the
column that gives us

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.880
the information about the time
point that we're predicting on

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:24.020
and the probability, except for

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:26.770
in this case, it's an NA. But
if you look at the other one,

00:12:26.770 --> 00:12:30.440
you do get actual probabilities.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:32.600
and.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:37.490
This does not return you 
a classical Surv object or survfit

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:39.660
object.

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.990
If you do want to access
the full survival curve,

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:48.090
you can pretty easily just dial
up the number of time points

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:49.170
you ask for.

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:53.190
So here I'm asking for survival
probability predicted at age

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:56.350
one all the way through 80.

00:12:56.350 --> 00:13:01.060
And then I can unnest that thing to
stack all the little tibbles

00:13:01.060 --> 00:13:02.180
underneath each other.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:06.910
And I'm squeezing in the mutate
to be still able to tell which

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:09.210
rows belong to which dolphin.

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:14.660
And that gives you a data
set that is ready for ggplot.

00:13:14.660 --> 00:13:19.160
And you can easily do your
visualization of the survival

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:23.030
curve with a geom_step.

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:25.920
So to recap.

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:28.800
The prediction
types that are new.

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:32.610
are "time" for survival
time and "survival"

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:34.850
for survival probability.

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:37.550
These are available for all of
the models that are in censored

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:39.720
and depending on the engine

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:42.980
you might also be able to
predict the hazard, the quantile

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:45.770
of the event time distribution
or the linear predictor

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:48.230
if your model has one.

00:13:48.230 --> 00:13:50.810
You do not have to do
any of the data prep

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:52.412
for a matrix interface.

00:13:52.412 --> 00:13:54.620
You don't have to pad your
results at the right place

00:13:54.620 --> 00:13:56.570
if there's a missing
value in there.

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:59.210
And you also don't
have to do the legwork

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:02.810
to go from a survival curve
to predicted probability

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:06.240
at any given time point.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:11.100
And the summary of this
all is basically: censored

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:14.800
is a consistent interface
for survival models.

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:16.410
It gives you
consistency in how you

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:20.220
specify and fit and consistency
in how you predict

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:21.250
and what you get back.

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:23.300
So please try it out.

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:35.690
