00:17:05.089 --> 00:17:08.829
I'll put a vignette on that

00:17:08.829 --> 00:17:12.900
online. Maybe one more question.

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:16.609
What is the difference between
stratifying - uh, that disappeared?

00:17:16.609 --> 00:17:18.359
What is the difference
between stratifying

00:17:18.359 --> 00:17:20.339
on a variable in
the survival model

00:17:20.339 --> 00:17:23.880
and using that variable
as a predictor?

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:29.670
So stratification in a Cox
model gives you the idea

00:17:29.670 --> 00:17:33.480
that the baseline hazard is
different between groups.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:36.750
And that's sort of the
non-parametric part,

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:40.110
the baseline and your predictors
are the parametric part

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:41.500
that modulate it.

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:46.740
And if you think that this
baseline hazard is not

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:49.950
proportional between groups but
rather fundamentally different

00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:54.480
than you would put that
in the stratification.

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:57.150
All right, let's give Hannah
another round of applause.

